(* tuvm#94 stage 5 — the ship's-bell clock as a RUN-ONCE deep-sleep appliance.

   THE REACTOR IS GONE. Stages 1-4 (MagTagClockNode.nsl, kept as the always-on demo
   sample) boot one VM and drain an event loop forever. Here, between wakes the CPU is
   off and THE VM DOES NOT EXIST: each timer wake is a fresh boot that lives a few
   seconds and dies. There is no drain loop, no channel, no VM timer — deepSleep: IS the
   idle point. This also DELETES the timer-instance bug class that cost the stage 2
   overnight run (a timer registered on one Timers, dispatched on another): there are no
   VM timers left to misregister.

   THE HALF-HOUR INDEX IS A MONOTONIC RATCHET, AND MONOTONIC MEANS MODULAR (design §2 as
   restated by §11). A strike is a pure increment, so a strike wake CANNOT double-bell. A
   sync may push the count FORWARD (skip missed strikes, silently) but NEVER backward —
   and "forward" is the FORWARD DISTANCE round the 48-slot dial, never `>`. Stated as an
   ordering it fails at midnight, where 47 and 0 change places: the 2026-07-24 run read a
   66 s lead as a 23.5 h lag and rang eight bells twice. When NTP says we are behind where
   we already struck, we hold the count and lengthen the next sleep — really lengthen it,
   out to the held count's OWN boundary, which is the other half of what §2 promised and
   the code did not do. Drift never touches the count, only the sleep duration (phase).

   THE COUNT OWNS THE SCHEDULE. Every sleep runs to the boundary of the next count, less
   the awake window, corrected by the persisted calibration. Those three terms fix three
   different errors — which boundary (§11 defect 5), the strike-to-sleep gap (§11 defect 6)
   and the RC oscillator's 1% rate (§11) — and the clock needs all three to ring on time.

   STATE LIVES IN .rtc.data (design §3), reached through rtcGet:/rtcPut:value:, because
   RAM does not survive the sleep. Field indices are the constants below and are
   identical to the C struct order in zephyr/src/main.c. Modulo is `\\` (rete#246),
   floored like `//`. *)
class MagTagDeepSleepNode packageUsing: manifest = ( | lastDriftSec syncOk basisMillis | ) (

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | plat half bells isWatchEnd |
    plat:: platform.

    (* Cold power-on leaves .rtc.data UNDEFINED (not zeroed), so a magic mismatch means
       "no valid state". A cold boot NEVER strikes and never falls through to the strike
       path below — it syncs, anchors, paints and sleeps to the next TRUE boundary
       (design §8, amended). *)
    (plat rtcValid) ifFalse: [ ^ self coldBoot: plat ].

    (* The strike about to happen: a pure increment of the ratchet (design §2). *)
    half:: (plat rtcGet: self rtcLastHalf) + 1.
    half = (86400 // self slotSec) ifTrue: [ half:: 0 ].  (* midnight wrap: last slot -> 0 *)
    bells:: (plat bell) bellsForHalfHour: half.           (* reuse the kernel's Bell method *)
    isWatchEnd:: bells = 8.                               (* 8 bells == a watch end *)

    (* Strike BLOCKS to completion before any sleep (design §8: strike never cut off). *)
    ('wake half ' , half printString , ' bells ' , bells printString) out.
    (plat bell) strike: bells.
    'struck' out.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcLastHalf value: half.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcBootTicks value: (plat rtcGet: self rtcBootTicks) + 1.

    (* Watch-end: sync + paint (Tasks 5-6). Stubbed here so the skeleton stands alone. *)
    isWatchEnd ifTrue: [ self onWatchEnd: plat ].

    ^ self sleepToCountBoundary: plat            (* NEVER RETURNS *)
  )

  (* THE NEXT WAKE BELONGS TO THE COUNT, NOT TO THE CLOCK (design §11, defect 5).
     The ratchet owns the schedule: having just struck `lastHalf`, the next strike is
     `lastHalf + 1`, and it is due at THAT half's boundary — so the sleep runs to
     ((lastHalf + 1) * slotSec) - predictedNow, mod 86400.

     This is the same number as "seconds to the next boundary" on the free-running path
     (count and clock are in step there), but it is DIFFERENT, and correct, after a sync
     that held the count: the sleep is LENGTHENED until the world reaches the count,
     which is what the class comment above has always promised. The previous spelling
     re-anchored to true time and ran to the next TRUE boundary, so a held count never
     re-converged and the gap grew one slot per watch end — the clock rang the wrong
     number of bells from 04:00 onward on the 2026-07-24 run.

     predictedNow = (anchor + elapsed) mod 86400 (design §3); cold/never-synced =>
     anchor 0, elapsed accumulates. *)
  sleepToCountBoundary: plat = (
    | predictedNow awakeSec sleepSec cal |
    predictedNow:: self predictedNow: plat.
    awakeSec:: self awakeSinceBasis.
    sleepSec:: (self secondsToCountBoundary: (plat rtcGet: self rtcLastHalf) from: predictedNow) - awakeSec.
    sleepSec < 1 ifTrue: [ sleepSec:: 1 ].    (* awake window longer than the slot *)
    cal:: plat rtcGet: self rtcCalPpm.
    (* Accrue the INTENDED REAL interval to the next wake: the target sleep PLUS this
       wake's awake window (boot + WiFi + strike, ~15-20 s on device). Without the awake
       term the predicted clock runs slow by that much per wake — ~1% at the 1800 s slot,
       which would swamp the RC-oscillator drift the panel exists to measure (~+9700 ppm of
       pure boot overhead), and 25-30% at a demo 60 s slot.

       The awake window is BOTH added here and subtracted from the sleep above, and it has
       to be both. Accruing it alone keeps the RATE honest but puts every strike one awake
       window past its boundary, because the seconds fall between this strike and the next;
       subtracting it alone would make the clock run fast. Together they hold the invariant
       that makes the bell land on the minute: predictedNow after this line is EXACTLY the
       boundary of the count about to be struck.

       This accrues `sleepSec`, NOT the disciplined value armed below. That is what keeps
       the correction from double-counting: the predicted clock advances by the real seconds
       we intend to pass, so the next sync measures only what the correction FAILED to
       remove, and calibration composes instead of oscillating. *)
    plat rtcPut: self rtcElapsedSec
         value: (plat rtcGet: self rtcElapsedSec) + sleepSec + awakeSec.
    ('sleep: target ' , sleepSec printString , ' s cal ' , (self signedField: cal)
        , ' awake ' , awakeSec printString , ' s') out.
    plat deepSleep: (self witnessSleepFor: (self disciplined: sleepSec ppm: cal)).  (* NEVER RETURNS *)
    ^ self )

  (* Seconds of awake work since the moment predictedNow's basis was established — NOT
     simply since boot. On a free-running wake those are the same thing: the clock comes
     from RTC state that was already current when the VM booted, so the basis is boot and
     `0 millis` is the answer.

     A SYNCED wake is different, and getting it wrong just moves the error. There,
     predictedNow is trueSec, which was true at the instant NTP answered — partway through
     the awake window, not at boot. Subtracting the whole window would over-correct by
     everything that happened before the NTP read (WiFi association is most of it), turning
     a 15 s late bell into a 15 s early one. trySyncUpTo3: stamps the basis at that instant,
     so only the work AFTER the read is subtracted. *)
  awakeSinceBasis = (
    | b |
    b:: basisMillis.
    b == nil ifTrue: [ b:: 0 ].       (* never synced this wake: the basis is boot *)
    ^ ((0 millis) - b) // 1000 )

  (* DISCIPLINE THE OSCILLATOR (design §11). Asleep, the sleep is counted by an internal
     ~90 kHz RC oscillator — this module has no 32.768 kHz crystal — and the 2026-07-24 run
     measured it at ~10,700 ppm FAST, 227x the navigator's 4 s/day, so bells landed up to
     155 s early between the 4 h syncs. The error is STABLE (10,500 / 10,768 / 10,271 across
     nine hours, +/-3%), and a stable rate error is correctable: command
     `target / (1 + cal/1e6)` so that `target` REAL seconds elapse. Only the ~3% variation
     survives, an estimated ~300 ppm — a watch going from 155 s of error to about 4 s, with
     no new parts. The re-anchor at each sync fixes the PHASE; this fixes the RATE.

     ROUNDED, not truncated, and that is not a nicety. The correction is whole seconds, so
     throwing the fraction away is itself a rate error: at the shipping slot a truncating
     spelling lost up to 0.9 s per 1800 s = 500 ppm, the same order as the residual this
     whole mechanism exists to remove, and it walked the bells ~30 s off across a watch.
     Rounding leaves +/-0.5 s per slot, so +/-4 s across a watch before the next sync
     re-anchors — which is the accuracy target. Sub-second correction would need fractional
     state across a deep sleep, and is what tuvm#100's hardware timebase buys instead.

     Everything stays inside a 31-bit SMI on device (|v| < 2^30). The direct product is
     used wherever it cannot overflow (target under 20000 caps it at 1e9); the rare long
     target — only a hold that waits out a multi-slot lead — takes an eighth-scaled form
     that caps at 5.4e8. The magnitude is divided rather than the signed value, so nothing
     depends on which way nsl rounds a negative quotient. *)
  disciplined: target ppm: cal = (
    | p mag corr |
    p:: cal.
    p >  50000 ifTrue: [ p::  50000 ].    (* a rate beyond 5% is a broken measurement, *)
    p < -50000 ifTrue: [ p:: -50000 ].    (* not an oscillator; refuse to act on it *)
    mag:: p.
    p < 0 ifTrue: [ mag:: 0 - p ].
    target < 20000
       ifTrue:  [ corr:: ((target * mag) + 500000) // 1000000 ]
       ifFalse: [ corr:: (((target // 8) * mag) + 62500) // 125000 ].
    p < 0 ifTrue: [ ^ target + corr ].             (* running fast -> command LONGER *)
    target - corr < 1 ifTrue: [ ^ 1 ].
    ^ target - corr )

  (* Seconds from `predicted` to the boundary of the count AFTER `lastHalf`, modular over
     the day. The single `+ 86400` is enough because predictedNow is already reduced to
     [0, 86400) and the target is at most 86400, so the difference cannot be more than one
     day negative — and doing it this way means no division of a negative number, whose
     rounding direction nsl does not pin down. *)
  secondsToCountBoundary: lastHalf from: predicted = (
    | d |
    d:: ((lastHalf + 1) * self slotSec) - predicted.
    d < 0 ifTrue: [ d:: d + 86400 ].
    d:: d \\ 86400.
    d < 1 ifTrue: [ d:: 1 ].             (* boundary already reached: wake at once *)
    ^ d )

  (* A COLD BOOT MUST NOT STRIKE. Power returns at an arbitrary second, and a bell struck
     then is a bell at a meaningless time — the 20:00:56 reset that produced "1 bell" is
     the witness. Instead: sync immediately so the clock starts on the right footing, let
     applySync: set the ratchet to true time (an advance from the free-run 0 — silent, no
     skipped bells rung), paint the synced panel, and sleep to the next TRUE boundary. The
     first strike is that boundary's bell with the RIGHT count.

     If NTP fails all 3 attempts we keep the free-run-from-00:00 state (design §8): the
     next boundary is 1800 s out and the count is wrong until the first good watch-end
     sync. Honest, and no worse than before. *)
  coldBoot: plat = (
    | trueSec |
    self initColdState: plat.
    trueSec:: self trySyncUpTo3: plat.
    trueSec == nil
       ifTrue:  [ syncOk:: false. self markSyncFailed: plat ]
       ifFalse: [ syncOk:: true.  self applySync: trueSec plat: plat ].
    (* One paint on cold boot, AFTER the sync so it shows the synced state (or FAIL):
       proves the display is alive without waiting up to a whole watch for the first
       watch-end paint, and replaces any stale image a reflash left on the glass. Cold
       boots are rare (true power loss / reflash), so this stays inside the e-paper
       refresh budget; ordinary strike wakes still never touch the panel (design §5). *)
    self paintStats: plat.
    ^ self sleepToCountBoundary: plat )

  (* Free-run from 00:00: predictedNow 0, ratchet 0 (so the first strike is half 1),
     staleSec large so the panel shows "never synced". Magic set LAST so a crash between
     writes cannot leave a half-initialised struct reading valid. The paint is NOT here —
     coldBoot: paints after the sync, so the panel never shows a state we already know is
     about to be corrected. *)
  initColdState: plat = (
    'cold boot: free-run from 00:00' out.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcLastHalf     value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcBootTicks    value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcAnchorSec    value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcElapsedSec   value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcCalPpm       value: self calPpmSeed.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcStaleSec     value: self neverSyncedSec.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Max   value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Min   value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcSyncsWin     value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcMagic        value: self magicWord.
    ^ self )

  onWatchEnd: plat = (
    | trueSec |
    trueSec:: self trySyncUpTo3: plat.
    trueSec == nil
       ifTrue:  [ syncOk:: false. self markSyncFailed: plat ]
       ifFalse: [ syncOk:: true.  self applySync: trueSec plat: plat ].
    self paintStats: plat.                    (* AFTER the sync, so a FAIL shows *)
    ^ self )

  (* 3 attempts fit one ~15 s awake window (design §4); "quit until next watch-end" is
     just: return nil, schedule nothing — the next attempt is 4 h on. sntp_simple's own
     4 s timeout spaces the retries (design §4: "3 × (4 s timeout + backoff)"); no extra
     backoff is spelled here (tuvm#97 has no sleep-spin primitive worth adding for it). *)
  trySyncUpTo3: plat = (
    | secs |
    3 timesRepeat: [
      secs:: plat ntpSyncTz: self tzOffset.
      (* Stamp the clock basis at the INSTANT true time was read — see awakeSinceBasis. *)
      secs == nil ifFalse: [ basisMillis:: 0 millis. ^ secs ].
      'ntp miss' out ].
    ^ nil )

  (* The §2 ratchet, both directions. NEVER moves the count backward; drift only ever
     adjusts the sleep (phase) via the re-anchored predictedNow next wake. *)
  applySync: trueSec plat: plat = (
    | trueHalf lastHalf predicted drift elapsed |
    trueHalf:: trueSec // self slotSec.
    lastHalf:: plat rtcGet: self rtcLastHalf.
    predicted:: self predictedAtSync: plat.

    ((self neverSynced: plat) or: [ self isBehind: trueHalf of: lastHalf ])
       ifTrue:  [ (* Fell behind (missed wakes / dead battery, or the clock ran slow):
                     advance SILENTLY — do NOT ring the skipped bells. `trueSec - predicted`
                     here is a CATCH-UP GAP, not oscillator drift (the board was off, so
                     elapsedSec never accrued those sleeps), so it is deliberately NOT
                     recorded — recording it would spike the 24 h drift window with a value
                     that is hours, not the seconds real drift produces. This also skips the
                     first-sync-after-cold-boot reset, which is almost always an advance. *)
                  ('sync true ' , trueSec printString , ' predicted ' , predicted printString , ' -> advance half ' , trueHalf printString) out.
                  plat rtcPut: self rtcLastHalf value: trueHalf ]
       ifFalse: [ (* On time / slightly ahead: HOLD the count and record the REAL drift —
                     the small signed gap between where the crystal put us and true time,
                     over the interval elapsedSec. This is the number the panel reports. *)
                  drift:: self wrapDrift: (trueSec - predicted).
                  lastDriftSec:: drift.
                  ('sync true ' , trueSec printString , ' predicted ' , predicted printString , ' -> hold half ' , lastHalf printString) out.
                  (* A RATE NEEDS A LONG ENOUGH BASELINE (design §11, defect 3). drift is
                     measured in whole seconds, so ppm carries +/-1e6/elapsed of pure
                     quantisation: 278 ppm over an hour, 14,700 ppm over a minute. The
                     2026-07-24 run resynced 68 s after the previous sync and turned a 4 s
                     error into `ppm +58823`. Report the sync (the ntp row still shows the
                     seconds), skip the rate — a number that bad is not a measurement, and
                     since disciplining now applies it to the SLEEP it would be a 5.9%
                     correction rather than a bad pixel. *)
                  elapsed:: plat rtcGet: self rtcElapsedSec.
                  elapsed < self driftFloorSec
                     ifTrue:  [ ('drift window ' , elapsed printString , ' s below floor ' , self driftFloorSec printString , ' s; ppm not recorded') out ]
                     ifFalse: [ self recordDrift: (self driftPpm: drift over: elapsed) plat: plat ] ].

    (* Re-anchor the wall clock to true time; zero the accumulated sleep + stale. *)
    plat rtcPut: self rtcAnchorSec  value: trueSec.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcElapsedSec value: 0.
    plat rtcPut: self rtcStaleSec   value: 0.
    ^ self )

  (* THE RATCHET INVARIANT IS MODULAR, NOT AN ORDERING (design §2 as restated by §11).
     The half index wraps 47 -> 0, so `trueHalf > lastHalf` is meaningless across midnight:
     on the 2026-07-24 run a 66 s LEAD (count 0, true 47) read as a 23.5 h LAG, the ratchet
     was shoved backward, and 8 bells rang a second time 63 s later.

     What is well defined is the FORWARD distance from the count to true time,
     `(trueHalf - lastHalf) mod slotsPerDay`. Small forward distance = we genuinely fell
     behind (missed wakes) -> advance. Distance 0 = on the count -> hold. Distance close to
     a full day = true time has not yet REACHED the count, i.e. we are running ahead of the
     world -> hold.

     "Close to a full day" is deliberately narrow (holdAheadSlots). Modular arithmetic
     cannot distinguish "3 slots ahead" from "45 slots behind", so the split is chosen by
     which mistake is survivable: advancing wrongly rings one wrong count and self-corrects
     next wake, while holding wrongly goes SILENT for the length of the hold. A lead can
     only be accumulated drift since the last sync — the worst ever measured is 155 s over
     a watch, well under one slot — so 3 slots is a 30x margin and still bounds a wrong
     hold to 2 hours. *)
  (* WHERE THE NODE'S CLOCK SAID IT WAS AT THE INSTANT NTP ANSWERED — the only thing
     trueSec may be compared against. predictedNow is the clock at the STRIKE; the sync
     happens an awake window later, so differencing the two scores boot and WiFi association
     as oscillator drift. At a 15 s window that is a phantom +1041 ppm per sync, which does
     not merely misreport: it feeds the calibration, so a PERFECT clock gets "corrected"
     every watch end and the loop walks away from the truth instead of converging on it. *)
  predictedAtSync: plat = (
    | p b |
    b:: basisMillis.
    b == nil ifTrue: [ b:: 0 ].
    p:: (self predictedNow: plat) + (b // 1000).
    ^ p \\ 86400 )

  (* A NEVER-SYNCED BOARD HAS NO RATCHET TO PROTECT, so its first good sync ADOPTS true
     time whatever the modular distance says. This is the one case where a large forward
     distance really is a lag: the free-run starts at 00:00, so a cold boot at 23:30 is a
     genuine 47 slots behind, which isBehind:of: would read as 1 slot ahead and hold —
     going silent until tomorrow. Every other way of falling hours behind is impossible,
     because a board that is merely failing NTP keeps ratcheting on its own timebase.

     `>=`, not `=`: markSyncFailed: keeps ADDING to the sentinel, so a cold boot whose NTP
     also failed reads 999999 + 14400 and must still count as never synced. *)
  neverSynced: plat = ( ^ (plat rtcGet: self rtcStaleSec) >= self neverSyncedSec )

  neverSyncedSec = ( ^ 999999 )

  isBehind: trueHalf of: lastHalf = (
    | n fwd |
    n:: self slotsPerDay.
    fwd:: trueHalf - lastHalf.
    fwd < 0 ifTrue: [ fwd:: fwd + n ].          (* both are in [0, n), so one add suffices *)
    fwd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ false ].                (* exactly on the count: hold and measure *)
    ^ fwd < (n - self holdAheadSlots) )

  markSyncFailed: plat = (
    plat rtcPut: self rtcStaleSec value: (plat rtcGet: self rtcStaleSec) + (self slotSec * 8).
    'sync failed, stale grows' out.
    ^ self )

  (* Two different numbers, easily confused, so name them apart:

     `ppm` here is the RESIDUAL measured this watch — how far the disciplined clock still
     missed by. It feeds the rolling 6-sync (24 h) extremes, reset at 6 (design §3), which
     is the honest "how good is this board now" readout.

     `rtcCalPpm` is the CUMULATIVE calibration actually applied when arming a sleep, so it
     ACCUMULATES the residual rather than being overwritten by it. Overwriting would undo
     the correction the moment it started working: a well-disciplined watch measures ~0,
     which as a replacement would set the calibration to zero and put the 1% error straight
     back. Composing two rates is a multiply, but at these magnitudes adding the ppm is
     accurate to ~3 ppm — far inside the residual — and the loop self-corrects anyway. *)
  recordDrift: ppm plat: plat = (
    | n mx mn |
    plat rtcPut: self rtcCalPpm value: (plat rtcGet: self rtcCalPpm) + ppm.
    n:: (plat rtcGet: self rtcSyncsWin) + 1.
    n >= 6 ifTrue: [ n:: 0 ].
    n = 0 ifTrue: [ plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Max value: ppm. plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Min value: ppm ]
          ifFalse: [
            mx:: plat rtcGet: self rtcDrift24Max. mn:: plat rtcGet: self rtcDrift24Min.
            ppm > mx ifTrue: [ plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Max value: ppm ].
            ppm < mn ifTrue: [ plat rtcPut: self rtcDrift24Min value: ppm ] ].
    plat rtcPut: self rtcSyncsWin value: n.
    ^ self )

  (* Drift wrapped across midnight: a true 0 against a predicted 86399 is +1 s, not
     -86399 (the same wrap the reactor's correctionTo: needed). *)
  wrapDrift: d = (
    d > 43200 ifTrue: [ ^ d - 86400 ].
    d < -43200 ifTrue: [ ^ d + 86400 ].
    ^ d )

  (* IDENTICAL spelling to 09_bell_math.nsl. *)
  driftPpm: driftSec over: elapsedSec = ( elapsedSec = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ 0 ]. ^ (driftSec * 1000000) // elapsedSec )

  tzOffset = ( ^ -25200 )   (* Phoenix, UTC-7, no DST — a product fact passed to the prim *)

  (* Witness seam (tuvm#94 stage 5, Task 9): the bench witness build flips this to
     `^ 15` so the PHYSICAL deep-sleep compresses to seconds and several strike wakes,
     a watch-end sync+paint, and a real deepSleep->timer-wake->cold-boot cycle all
     witness within minutes. The CLOCK still advances a real half-hour per wake —
     elapsedSec accrues the true `sleepSec` above, unchanged — so the ratchet and
     watch-end (half 8) land exactly as shipped; only the wall-clock wait shortens.
     SHIPPING state is this pass-through (the aux gate asserts the 1800 s arming). *)
  witnessSleepFor: realSec = ( ^ realSec )

  (* Painted ONLY at watch-ends (design §5), AFTER the sync, so a FAIL shows. Per-row
     best-effort: each row draws inside its own catch, so an over-wide value (e.g. the
     16 MB host arena's heap row) skips just that row instead of blanking the panel.
     A whole-panel guard around `display`/`clear` still carries on headless if the board
     has no panel at all. *)
  paintStats: plat = (
    | disp hs |
    (* Sentinel-and-return: a handler block is a real escaping closure, so `^` inside it
       is a compile error under the no-NLR model (review/lib/errors.md) — capture the
       on:do: result (the display, or the nil sentinel from the handler) and `^` from the
       method body instead. *)
    disp:: [ | d | d:: plat display. d clear. d ]
      on: [ :e | e tag = #displayUnavailable ]
      do: [ :e | nil ].
    disp == nil ifTrue: [ 'no display; skipping paint' out. ^ self ].
    hs:: plat heapStats.
    self tryRow: 0 s: (self line: 'oevm' val: '')                                       on: disp.
    self tryRow: 1 s: '----------'                                                      on: disp.
    self tryRow: 2 s: (self line: 'heap' val: (self heapField: hs))                     on: disp.
    self tryRow: 3 s: (self line: 'ppm'  val: (self signedField: (plat rtcGet: self rtcCalPpm))) on: disp.
    self tryRow: 4 s: (self line: 'up'   val: (self upField: plat))                     on: disp.
    self tryRow: 5 s: '----------'                                                      on: disp.
    self tryRow: 6 s: (self line: 'ntp'  val: self ntpField)                            on: disp.
    self tryRow: 7 s: (self line: '24h'  val: (self signedField: (plat rtcGet: self rtcDrift24Max))) on: disp.
    self tryRow: 8 s: (self line: ''     val: (self signedField: (plat rtcGet: self rtcDrift24Min))) on: disp.
    self tryRow: 9 s: (self line: 'sync' val: (self staleField: (plat rtcGet: self rtcStaleSec)))    on: disp.
    [ disp refresh ] on: [ :e | (e tag = #displayFault) or: [ e tag = #displayUnavailable ] ]
                     do: [ :e | ('refresh skipped: ' , e tag printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  (* Draw one row and echo it, catching a too-wide/faulted row so the rest still paint.
     The echo is INSIDE the try and AFTER the draw, so a skipped row emits no `| ` line —
     which is exactly what the host test keys on for the (skipped) heap row. *)
  tryRow: r s: s on: disp = (
    [ disp at: r col: 0 put: s. ('| ' , s) out ]
      on: [ :e | (e tag = #displayBounds) or: [ e tag = #displayFault ] ]
      do: [ :e | ('row ' , r printString , ' skipped: ' , e tag printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  (* live rounded to whole KiB / arena in KiB. On device (20 KB) this is `20/20K`; on the
     host image VM (16 MB) it is `.../16384K`, which overflows the 12-col grid and is
     skipped by tryRow: — the row is witnessed on device (Task 9), not on host. *)
  heapField: hs = ( ^ (self kRound: hs liveBytes) , '/' , (hs heapSize // 1024) printString , 'K' )

  (* The `ntp` row: never attempted (cold-boot placeholder paint) -> `--`; this watch
     FAILED -> FAIL; synced and measured drift (hold branch) -> `+N.Ns`; synced but
     advanced (missed wakes, no drift measured) -> `sync`. *)
  ntpField = (
    syncOk == nil ifTrue: [ ^ '--' ].
    syncOk ifFalse: [ ^ 'FAIL' ].
    lastDriftSec == nil ifTrue: [ ^ 'sync' ].
    ^ (self signedField: lastDriftSec) , '.0s' )

  (* Uptime from bootTicks (one slot per tick), wrap-safe — the reactor's upField with
     the cadence derived from slotSec. *)
  upField: plat = (
    | mins d r h m |
    mins:: (plat rtcGet: self rtcBootTicks) * (self slotSec // 60).
    d:: mins // 1440.
    r:: mins - (d * 1440).
    h:: r // 60.
    m:: r - (h * 60).
    ^ d printString , 'd' , (self pad: h) , ':' , (self pad: m) )

  (* Age since last good sync, `Nd/NNh` — IDENTICAL spelling to 09_bell_math.nsl.
     The cold-init sentinel (999999, "never synced") renders as `--`, not as a
     meaningless 11-day age. *)
  staleField: sec = (
    | mins d r h |
    sec >= self neverSyncedSec ifTrue: [ ^ '--' ].
    mins:: sec // 60.
    d:: mins // 1440.
    r:: mins - (d * 1440).
    h:: r // 60.
    ^ d printString , 'd/' , (self pad: h) , 'h' )

  (* Signed integer, `+N` / `-N`. nsl has no unary minus display, so build it. *)
  signedField: n = (
    n < 0 ifTrue: [ ^ '-' , (0 - n) printString ].
    ^ '+' , n printString )

  (* Right-justify `v` against `label` in exactly 12 columns; overshoot (pad < 0) is left
     un-truncated so the too-wide row raises #displayBounds and tryRow: skips it. *)
  line: label val: v = (
    | pad |
    pad:: 12 - label size - v size.
    pad < 0 ifTrue: [ pad:: 0 ].
    ^ label , (self spaces: pad) , v )

  spaces: n = ( | s | s:: ''. n timesRepeat: [ s:: s , ' ' ]. ^ s )
  kRound: bytes = ( ^ ((bytes + 512) // 1024) printString )
  pad: n = ( (n < 10) ifTrue: [ ^ '0' , n printString ]. ^ n printString )

  predictedNow: plat = (
    | now |
    now:: (plat rtcGet: self rtcAnchorSec) + (plat rtcGet: self rtcElapsedSec).
    ^ now \\ 86400 )

  (* THE CADENCE. One slot = one bell tick; a "watch" is 8 slots; the day is
     86400//slotSec slots. SHIPPING is 1800 (the real ship's-bell half-hour). A demo
     build may flip this to 60 (bells on real minute marks, sync+paint every 8 min —
     still beyond the 180 s e-paper guidance) and everything scales: boundary math,
     trueHalf, the stale accrual, the up row, the midnight wrap. The math is honest at
     any slot ONLY because elapsedSec accrues awake time too — without that term a
     small slot runs visibly slow (25-30% at 60 s). *)
  slotSec = ( ^ 1800 )

  slotsPerDay = ( ^ 86400 // self slotSec )

  (* How far the count may legitimately lead true time before a sync reads it as a lag
     instead (see isBehind:of:). Three slots; the worst lead ever measured is 155 s. *)
  holdAheadSlots = ( ^ 3 )

  (* Shortest baseline that yields a believable ppm. Two slots = an hour at the shipping
     cadence, which is David's "an hour is defensible" and bounds the whole-second
     quantisation to +/-278 ppm — the same order as the residual disciplining aims for.
     Derived from slotSec rather than hardcoded so a 60 s demo build can still measure and
     discipline itself (floor 120 s) instead of never recording a rate at all. *)
  driftFloorSec = ( ^ self slotSec * 2 )

  (* FACTORY CALIBRATION — the calibration a never-synced board starts from, so the very
     first sleep after a reflash is already disciplined instead of running a whole 4 h watch
     at 155 s of error before the first sync has a number to work with. This is the mean of
     the three measurements from the 2026-07-24/25 overnight run on THIS board
     (10,500 / 10,768 / 10,271 ppm fast; negative here because a fast oscillator makes
     predicted time outrun true time). It is a starting guess, not a constant: the first
     watch-end sync composes the measured residual onto it, so a wrong seed self-heals
     within one watch, and a board with a different RC part converges just as fast.
     Retires when the DS3231M (tuvm#100, +/-5 ppm) takes over the timebase. *)
  calPpmSeed = ( ^ -10513 )

  (* NOTE: bellsForHalfHour: is NOT redefined here — the node calls the kernel's
     Bell>>bellsForHalfHour: via `(plat bell) bellsForHalfHour: half`, exactly as the
     reactor does (MagTagClockNode.nsl:250). *)

  (* --- RTC field indices (design §3 table; identical order to the C struct) ------ *)
  rtcMagic      = ( ^ 0 )
  rtcLastHalf   = ( ^ 1 )
  rtcBootTicks  = ( ^ 2 )
  rtcAnchorSec  = ( ^ 3 )
  rtcElapsedSec = ( ^ 4 )
  rtcCalPpm     = ( ^ 5 )
  rtcStaleSec   = ( ^ 6 )
  rtcDrift24Max = ( ^ 7 )
  rtcDrift24Min = ( ^ 8 )
  rtcSyncsWin   = ( ^ 9 )
  magicWord     = ( ^ 348000704 )   (* 0x14BE11C0 — MUST fit a 32-bit SMI (31-bit
     tag, so |value| < 2^30). The original 0x94BE11C0 overflowed: it round-tripped
     on the 64-bit host but stored garbage through rtcPut: on the device, so
     rtcValid was false on every wake and the clock cold-booted forever. *)
) : (
)
